SECTION 2 — RED BASELINE: NO SERVER-SIDE TLS EXISTS AT HEAD
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

2.1 — Baseline build green BEFORE any probe, so probe failure is attributable to
      missing TLS and not to a stale or broken binary.

    $ cd engine && cargo build -p flapjack-server 2>&1 | tail -30
    ...
       Compiling flapjack-http v1.0.10 (engine/flapjack-http)
       Compiling flapjack-replication v1.0.10 (engine/flapjack-replication)
       Compiling flapjack-server v1.0.10 (engine/flapjack-server)
        Finished `dev` profile [unoptimized + debuginfo] target(s) in 3m 43s
    exit 0; produced engine/target/debug/flapjack

2.2 — Source-level proof that no TLS acceptor exists (checklist-mandated grep):

    $ cd engine && grep -rn "rustls\|TlsAcceptor" flapjack-http/src/ flapjack-server/src/
    exit=1
    (zero matching lines — empty result is the evidence)

    Note: `rustls` DOES appear in the dependency tree (Cargo.lock has
    rustls 0.23.40, tokio-rustls 0.26.4, hyper-rustls 0.27.9), pulled in as a
    CLIENT-side TLS stack by reqwest/rust-s3/instant-acme. Nothing in the server
    crates references it. See Section 3.4.

2.3 — Behavioral RED probe. Launch command (isolated temp data dir, ephemeral
      port, auth off so the probe needs no key):

    $ TMPD=$(mktemp -d)
    $ engine/target/debug/flapjack --data-dir "$TMPD/data" --auto-port --no-auth \
        > "$TMPD/server.log" 2>&1 &

    Resolved port for this run: 51845 (from the startup banner line
    `->  Local:      http://127.0.0.1:51845`).

    Liveness control — the server IS up and answering on that exact port:

    $ curl -sS -o /dev/null -w 'HTTP %{http_code} scheme=%{scheme}\n' \
        http://127.0.0.1:51845/health
    HTTP 200 scheme=HTTP

    This control matters: without it, a failed handshake could just mean
    "nothing listening". It proves the same socket serves plaintext HTTP and
    refuses TLS.

2.4 — PROBE 1 (checklist / operator-directive form):

    $ openssl s_client -connect 127.0.0.1:51845 </dev/null
    8717349120:error:1404B42E:SSL routines:ST_CONNECT:tlsv1 alert protocol version:.../tls13_lib.c:151:
    CONNECTED(00000003)
    ---
    no peer certificate available
    ---
    No client certificate CA names sent
    ---
    SSL handshake has read 5 bytes and written 294 bytes
    ---
    New, (NONE), Cipher is (NONE)
    Secure Renegotiation IS NOT supported
    Compression: NONE
    Expansion: NONE
    No ALPN negotiated
    SSL-Session:
        Protocol  : TLSv1.3
        Cipher    : 0000
        Session-ID:
        Master-Key:
    ---
    probe1_exit=1

    Reading: TCP connect succeeds (`CONNECTED`), then the handshake dies.
    "no peer certificate available", `Cipher is (NONE)`, and a nonzero exit are
    the RED assertions. The 5 bytes read back are the plain-HTTP server's
    response bytes being misread as a TLS record header, which is exactly the
    "plaintext listener, no terminator" signature.

2.5 — PROBE 2 (SNI variant):

    $ openssl s_client -connect 127.0.0.1:51845 -servername localhost </dev/null
    8717349120:error:1404B42E:SSL routines:ST_CONNECT:tlsv1 alert protocol version:.../tls13_lib.c:151:
    CONNECTED(00000003)
    ---
    no peer certificate available
    ---
    SSL handshake has read 5 bytes and written 312 bytes
    ---
    New, (NONE), Cipher is (NONE)
    No ALPN negotiated
    ---
    probe2_exit=1

    WHY THE SNI VARIANT MATTERS HERE: identical failure, and that identity is
    the point. It proves the current failure is "no TLS terminator at all", not
    "a terminator that rejected this server name". Stage 2 ships a single
    static cert/key pair with no SNI-based cert selection, so once TLS lands,
    Probe 1 and Probe 2 must BOTH succeed and must both present the same
    certificate. A future Stage-2/3 result where Probe 2 succeeds and Probe 1
    fails would mean an SNI-required config was introduced — a regression
    against the loopback/IP-address use case this lane targets, since
    `openssl s_client -connect 127.0.0.1:<port>` sends no SNI at all.

2.6 — Client tool identity (LibreSSL, not OpenSSL — macOS system default):

    $ openssl version
    LibreSSL 3.3.6

    Stage 3's probe script must not assume GNU/OpenSSL-only flags or error
    strings. LibreSSL 3.3.6's failure text here is `tlsv1 alert protocol
    version`; OpenSSL 3.x typically emits `wrong version number` for the same
    condition. Assert on handshake failure + absence of a peer certificate +
    nonzero exit, NOT on the vendor-specific error string.

2.7 — Graceful-shutdown behavior that must survive Stage 2 (captured from the
      same run's server log at SIGTERM):

    INFO flapjack_http::startup: [shutdown] Received SIGTERM
    INFO flapjack_http::server: [shutdown] Server stopped accepting connections, cleaning up... timeout_env_var="FLAPJACK_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_SECS" timeout_secs=30
    INFO flapjack_http::server: [shutdown] Analytics buffers flushed
    INFO flapjack::analytics::collector: [analytics] Flush loop shutting down
    INFO flapjack_http::server: [shutdown] All write queues drained before deadline timeout_env_var="FLAPJACK_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_SECS" timeout_secs=30

    These five lines are the plaintext GREEN reference for the drain path.
    Stage 2's TLS path must emit the same sequence, in particular
    "All write queues drained before deadline". Its failure twin —
    "Write queue drain timed out; forcing exit may drop queued writes
    (data-loss risk)" — is the data-loss condition named in the Stage 2
    preserve block.

