SECTION 3 — OWNER MAP (traced at HEAD)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

3.1 — CLI / config owners Stage 2 must EXTEND (not duplicate):

  `engine/flapjack-server/src/main.rs`
    - `struct Cli` (line 20). Every option is `#[arg(long, env = "FLAPJACK_...")]`.
      This is where `--ssl-cert-path` / `--ssl-key-path` belong.
    - `resolve_runtime_config()` (line 265) -> `resolve_data_dir()` (274),
      `resolve_bind_addr()` (285). Precedence is resolved HERE, then handed to
      the http crate via env. `resolve_bind_addr` already models the exact
      pattern Stage 2 needs for conflict rejection: `--auto-port` + `--bind-addr`
      returns `Err("--auto-port cannot be used with --bind-addr")` (line 290).
      The both-or-neither TLS check should follow that shape and be a sibling
      `resolve_tls_config()` returning `Result<_, String>` — a pure function,
      unit-testable without binding a socket.

  `engine/flapjack-http/src/startup.rs`
    - `ServerConfig` + `load_server_config()` (line 350). This is the SINGLE
      env-to-struct reader for the http crate (`FLAPJACK_ENV`,
      `FLAPJACK_NO_AUTH`, `FLAPJACK_DATA_DIR`, `FLAPJACK_BIND_ADDR`, ...). TLS
      paths must land on `ServerConfig` here. Do NOT add a second `std::env::var`
      call inside `server.rs`.
    - `print_startup_banner()` (line 748) — ADJACENT SINGLE-SOURCE OWNER, and
      the easiest thing to silently break. Line 757 hardcodes
      `let url = format!("http://{}", bind_addr);` and lines 774/780 derive the
      dashboard and swagger-ui URLs from that same `url`. Ship TLS without
      touching this and the banner advertises three `http://` links against an
      HTTPS-only socket. Stage 2 must thread a scheme through this one function
      so all three lines flip together; it must not add a second banner path.
      Upstream Meilisearch has this exact bug filed
      (meilisearch/meilisearch#2660, "Address in the launch resume is always
      http even if https is used") — do not inherit it.
    - `shutdown_signal()` — the shutdown future the plaintext path awaits.

3.2 — Listener owner (`engine/flapjack-http/src/server.rs`, `serve()`):

    let listener = tokio::net::TcpListener::bind(&infrastructure.bind_addr).await?;
    let auth_status = resolve_auth_status(&server_config, key_is_new, admin_key);
    print_startup_banner(&listener.local_addr()?.to_string(), auth_status, ...);

    axum::serve(
        listener,
        app.into_make_service_with_connect_info::<std::net::SocketAddr>(),
    )
    .with_graceful_shutdown(shutdown_signal())
    .await?;

    let _ = run_graceful_shutdown(&mut infrastructure, &state, shutdown_timeout_secs).await;
    Ok(())

  That is the entire block to swap. Three properties are load-bearing:
    (a) `listener.local_addr()` must still be read BEFORE serving, because
        `--auto-port` binds :0 and the banner (and every test that scrapes the
        banner for the port, including the RED probe above) depends on it.
    (b) `into_make_service_with_connect_info::<SocketAddr>()` is what makes
        `ConnectInfo<SocketAddr>` extractable.
    (c) `run_graceful_shutdown(...)` runs AFTER the serve future returns. It is
        a separate statement, not chained — so the TLS arm must return control
        to this same point rather than owning its own exit.

3.3 — SSL owner is ACME state, NOT socket termination. Confirmed:

  `engine/flapjack-http/src/server_init.rs`
    - `InfrastructureState.ssl_manager: Option<Arc<flapjack::SslManager>>` (36)
    - `initialize_ssl_manager()` (396): builds an `SslManager` from
      `SslConfig::from_env()`, registers it globally, returns `None` when
      `FLAPJACK_SSL_EMAIL` is unset. Never touches a socket.
  `engine/flapjack-ssl/src/manager.rs`
    - `struct SslManager` (11) holds `config`, `acme_client`, `last_check`,
      `last_renewal`, `renewal_status`. There is NO in-memory certificate and NO
      key field. Public API is `new`, `start_renewal_loop`, `get_acme_client`,
      `get_status`.
  `engine/flapjack-http/src/handlers/internal.rs`
    - `acme_challenge()` (972) serves `/.well-known/acme-challenge/:token` over
      PLAIN HTTP via `get_challenge_response`. ADJACENT OWNER TO PRESERVE:
      HTTP-01 validation is defined to happen over port 80 plaintext. Stage 2
      must not make this route unreachable, and Stage 4 must not move it behind
      TLS.

  FINDING RELEVANT TO STAGE 4 (recorded now so Stage 4 does not re-derive it):
  the existing ACME path CANNOT feed an in-binary terminator without a change.
    - `manager.rs:126` — `let (cert_pem, _key_pem) = acme_client.request_certificate(...)`.
      The private key is DISCARDED. `AcmeClient::request_certificate`
      (`engine/flapjack-ssl/src/acme.rs:55`) returns `(certificate_pem,
      private_key_pem)`, but only the cert survives.
    - `manager.rs:153` `write_certificate_files()` writes only `fullchain.pem`
      to the hardcoded absolute path `/etc/letsencrypt/live/<ip>/`.
    - `manager.rs:172` `reload_nginx()` shells out to `systemctl reload nginx`.
  In other words the current ACME implementation assumes an EXTERNAL nginx
  terminator holding a key it never sees. Stage 4's "can SslManager feed the
  selected terminator without expanding this lane?" question therefore starts
  from a NO on the current code: it needs key retention plus a non-root-owned
  storage location plus a reload hook that is not `systemctl`. Stage 4 should
  weigh that against filing the gap spec.

3.4 — Dependency reachability (checked before recommending anything new):

  Already in `engine/Cargo.lock`, so no new tree:
    rustls 0.23.40, rustls-pki-types 1.14.1, tokio-rustls 0.26.4,
    hyper 1.9.0, hyper-util 0.1.20, ring 0.17.14, rcgen 0.13.2 (+0.14.8)
  NOT present:
    rustls-pemfile (0 entries), axum-server (0 entries)
  Direct declarations today: no workspace crate declares tokio-rustls; rustls
  arrives only transitively through reqwest `rustls-tls`, rust-s3
  `tokio-rustls-tls`, and flapjack-ssl's instant-acme `hyper-rustls`/`ring`.

  So a tokio-rustls-based seam adds ONE genuinely new crate (`rustls-pemfile`,
  a tiny PEM parser) and promotes already-compiled crates to direct deps. An
  `axum-server` seam would add a new crate plus its own server stack.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 4 — STAGE 2 SEAM DECISION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

4.0 — THE HARD CONSTRAINT (this is what decides the stage):

  axum 0.7.9's `serve` is hard-bound to a concrete `tokio::net::TcpListener`.
  Verified in the vendored crate source, `axum-0.7.9/src/serve.rs:92`:

      pub fn serve<M, S>(tcp_listener: TcpListener, make_service: M) -> Serve<M, S>

  and `axum-0.7.9/src/serve.rs:532`:

      pub struct IncomingStream<'a> {
          tcp_stream: &'a TokioIo<TcpStream>,
          remote_addr: SocketAddr,
      }

  There is no `Listener` trait in axum 0.7 (that arrived in axum 0.8). A
  `tokio_rustls::server::TlsStream` therefore CANNOT be handed to
  `axum::serve`. "Just wrap the listener and keep calling axum::serve" is not
  available. Any plan that assumes it will fail at Stage 2 compile time.

4.1 — THE ENABLING FACT (this is what makes the chosen seam cheap):

  `axum-0.7.9/src/extract/connect_info.rs:97`:

      impl Connected<SocketAddr> for SocketAddr {
          fn connect_info(remote_addr: SocketAddr) -> Self { remote_addr }
      }

  and line 103:

      impl<S, C, T> Service<T> for IntoMakeServiceWithConnectInfo<S, C>
      where S: Clone, C: Connected<T>

  Consequence: the value returned by
  `app.into_make_service_with_connect_info::<SocketAddr>()` is a `Service` over
  BOTH `IncomingStream<'_>` (what `axum::serve` feeds it) AND a bare
  `SocketAddr`. A hand-rolled TLS accept loop can call
  `make_service.call(peer_addr)` and get back the identical
  `AddExtension<Router, ConnectInfo<SocketAddr>>` that the plaintext path
  produces. Connect-info is preserved by construction, using the same
  expression, not by a parallel re-implementation.

4.2 — DECISION: extract one small TLS-serve seam inside `flapjack-http`
      (e.g. `engine/flapjack-http/src/tls_serve.rs`), and have `serve()` choose
      between the existing `axum::serve(...)` arm and the new arm at the single
      existing call site.

  Shape:
    - `serve()` keeps binding ONE `tokio::net::TcpListener` and keeps calling
      `print_startup_banner(&listener.local_addr()?...)` before serving, so
      `--auto-port` and banner behavior are untouched. Only the scheme argument
      to the banner changes.
    - `let make_service = app.into_make_service_with_connect_info::<SocketAddr>();`
      is hoisted so BOTH arms consume the same expression (4.1).
    - Plaintext arm: unchanged `axum::serve(listener, make_service)
      .with_graceful_shutdown(shutdown_signal()).await?`.
    - TLS arm: `tokio_rustls::TlsAcceptor` over the same listener; per
      connection `make_service.call(peer_addr)` -> `TowerToHyperService` ->
      `hyper_util::server::conn::auto::Builder::serve_connection_with_upgrades`;
      accept loop selects against `shutdown_signal()` and drains in-flight
      connections with `hyper_util::server::graceful::GracefulShutdown`.
    - `run_graceful_shutdown(...)` stays OUTSIDE the match, exactly where it is
      today. Both arms fall through to it, so the drain cannot be skipped by
      construction rather than by remembering to call it.
  New deps: `tokio-rustls` + `rustls` + `rustls-pemfile` (+ `hyper-util`
  features `server-auto`, `server-graceful`, `service`) on
  `engine/flapjack-http/Cargo.toml`. Only rustls-pemfile is new to the lock
  (3.4). Stage 2 must install a process-level rustls crypto provider explicitly
  (`rustls::crypto::ring::default_provider().install_default()`); rustls 0.23
  panics at `ServerConfig` build time if none is set, and `ring` is already the
  provider in tree via instant-acme.

  WHY THIS IS CLEANER THAN A PARALLEL SERVER PATH: the risk this lane is
  actually exposed to is DUPLICATE OWNERSHIP of two things the Stage 2 preserve
  block names — connect-info and the write-queue drain. A parallel
  `serve_tls()` entry point would fork the whole startup sequence (config load,
  auth validation, key store, infrastructure init, pre-serve barrier,
  background tasks, banner) and give the drain a second caller that can rot
  independently. Confining the fork to the ~15 lines between "listener bound"
  and "serve future returned" means startup, banner, and shutdown each keep
  exactly one owner. The TLS arm is a listener detail, not a second server.

4.3 — ALTERNATIVE CONSIDERED AND REJECTED: `axum-server` crate with its
      `tls-rustls` feature.
  Rejected on ownership, not on capability. `axum-server` drives graceful
  shutdown through its own `Handle` type (`handle.graceful_shutdown(Some(dur))`)
  rather than through a shutdown future. Adopting it means either the existing
  `shutdown_signal()` in `engine/flapjack-http/src/startup.rs` stops being the
  single shutdown owner, or a bridging task is added whose only job is to
  translate one shutdown mechanism into another — a second owner for the exact
  invariant the Stage 2 preserve block says must not be lost. It also brings a
  whole crate that is not in the lock today (3.4) to do what a bounded amount of
  already-compiled hyper-util code does, and its `Accept` abstraction would have
  to be re-checked for connect-info behavior anyway.

4.4 — ALTERNATIVE CONSIDERED AND REJECTED: upgrade axum 0.7 -> 0.8 to use the
      new `Listener` trait, which would let `axum::serve` take a TLS listener
      directly.
  This is the technically tidiest end state and it is far outside this lane. It
  is a breaking upgrade across `flapjack` (feature `axum-support`),
  `flapjack-http` (every handler, extractor, and the utoipa/utoipa-swagger-ui
  integration), and `flapjack-server`. This lane's `ROADMAP.md` row is an
  optional listener flag, and its own text says "the flag is an option, not a
  new blessed path". Trading that for a workspace-wide framework migration
  inverts the cost. Recorded here so a later reviewer sees it was weighed, not
  missed.

